    "result_text": "The acceptance suite is the entire test set, and all 42 tests pass. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional, city-region-validated postcodes on owner creation for this Spring Boot PetClinic app.\n\n**Changes made:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added an optional `postcode` property to the `OwnerFields` schema with `pattern: '^[0-9]{4}$'`. Because it lives in `OwnerFields`, it's both writable (create) and returned (via the `Owner` `allOf`). The 4-digit pattern gives an automatic **400** for malformed postcodes (e.g. `\"12\"`) through the existing bean-validation \u2192 `MethodArgumentNotValidException` \u2192 advice path.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `postcode` field (`@Column(name = \"postcode\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it automatically in both directions by name.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added a fixed `REGION_POSTCODES` table (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099) and an `isPostcodeValidForCity(...)` helper. In `addOwner`, when a postcode is present it's checked against the range for the region derived from the city (reusing the existing `Owner.getLocality()` city\u2192region mapping); out-of-range \u2192 **400**. A `null` postcode or an `UNKNOWN` region (unmapped city) is accepted.\n\n4. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 Added the `postcode` column. Tests run under the hsqldb profile with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema column was required there; the others were updated for consistency.\n\n**Behavior matches the spec:** validated only when present, optional/backward-compatible when absent, region-range enforced with 400 rejection, permissive default for unknown cities, and stored/returned when given.",
